Title: Stack Exchange about the opinion: Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp.

### Why does the Federal Circuit have appellate jurisdiction over this TTAB decision instead of a regional circuit?
Question:
The opinion cites 28 U.S.C. § 1295(a)(4)(B) for jurisdiction. I thought most trademark appeals went to regional circuits. Can someone explain the statutory pathway that sends this TTAB opposition appeal to the Federal Circuit (e.g., how § 1295(a)(4)(B) interacts with 15 U.S.C. § 1071(a) vs. § 1071(b)) and whether Apex could have elected a different route (a civil action) that would have changed the forum and standard of review?
Answer:
Short answer
The Federal Circuit has jurisdiction because Apex appealed the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board’s (TTAB) decision as an agency appeal, and the Federal Circuit’s appellate jurisdiction over TTAB appeals is grounded in 28 U.S.C. § 1295(a)(4)(B). The opinion makes this explicit and applies the Federal Circuit’s usual review framework for TTAB appeals (de novo review of legal conclusions; substantial‑evidence review of fact findings). Apex could have pursued a parallel statutory route — a civil action in a district court under the Lanham Act — but it did not; electing that route would have put the case in a regional district court and would have produced a different (effectively de novo) factual and evidentiary proceeding.

The statutory pathway (how the pieces fit)
- The opinion states jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. § 1295(a)(4)(B). That statute is the source of the Federal Circuit’s jurisdiction over certain appeals from the Patent and Trademark Office (including TTAB proceedings). The court does not restate the full statutory text, but that is the provision the opinion cites as the basis for hearing this appeal (Opinion, “We have jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. § 1295(a)(4)(B).”).
- The opinion also sets out the governing review standard for TTAB appeals: “We review the [B]oard’s legal conclusions de novo, and its findings of fact for substantial evidence.” (Opinion § II (quoting M2 Software, Inc. v. M2 Commc’ns, Inc.).) That is the practical consequence of choosing the agency‑appeal route to the Federal Circuit: the Board’s findings of fact stand unless supported by substantial evidence, and the court reviews legal conclusions anew.

How § 1295(a)(4)(B) interacts with the Lanham Act routes (briefly)
- The Lanham Act contemplates two different procedural routes when parties dispute registrability through TTAB proceedings:
  1. Appeal the TTAB decision to the Court of Appeals (the Federal Circuit in cases covered by § 1295(a)(4)(B)). That is the route Apex used and is reflected in this opinion.
  2. Alternatively, a party may bring a civil action in federal district court (a regional circuit forum) to determine registrability — that is a separate, statutory option under the Lanham Act. Because the opinion does not reproduce the Lanham Act text, it doesn’t catalogue the statutory subsections, but the practical result is well illustrated here: choosing the agency appeal invokes § 1295(a)(4)(B) and the Federal Circuit’s appellate standards; choosing a civil action would place the dispute in a district court.

Effect of an election on forum and standard of review
- Forum: Apex’s choice to appeal placed the case in the Federal Circuit. If Apex had instead brought a civil action in a district court, it would have litigated in that regional court (and any subsequent appeal from the district court would go to the regional circuit court of appeals).
- Standard/procedure: The opinion explains the consequence of the chosen route: the Federal Circuit reviews the Board’s legal conclusions de novo and factual findings for substantial evidence (Opinion § II). By contrast, a district‑court civil action would be a fresh adjudication of registrability, involving de novo factfinding on whatever evidence the parties present in that proceeding (i.e., the district court considers the evidence anew rather than deferring to the Board’s factual findings under the substantial‑evidence standard). The opinion itself highlights the appellate standard it applied; it therefore implicitly contrasts with the more plenary fact‑finding posture of district‑court litigation by showing the Federal Circuit’s role in reviewing an administrative record (see Opinion § II).

Practical takeaway
- The Federal Circuit’s jurisdiction in this case flows from Apex’s appellate choice and § 1295(a)(4)(B), and the opinion applies the familiar Federal Circuit review framework for TTAB appeals (de novo law; substantial‑evidence fact review).
- Apex could have elected the district‑court route instead (a civil action under the Lanham Act) and thereby litigated in a regional court with de novo factfinding — but it chose the agency‑appeal route, which is why the Federal Circuit heard the case and applied the standards set out in Section II of the opinion.

### What satisfies Article III standing for an applicant appealing a TTAB § 2(d) refusal?
Question:
The court doesn’t discuss standing explicitly. As the losing applicant, does Apex automatically have Article III standing to appeal to the Federal Circuit just because registration was refused, or does it need to show some additional injury-in-fact beyond the denial of registration? How does this compare to (1) an opposer’s entitlement to bring an opposition before the TTAB (post-Lexmark framing under 15 U.S.C. § 1063) and (2) standing requirements the Federal Circuit imposes in appeals from the PTAB in patent cases?
Answer:
Short answer
The opinion does not discuss Article III standing as a threshold issue. It treats Apex as an aggrieved party (Apex “appeals” and the court states jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. § 1295(a)(4)(B)) and proceeds to decide the merits. From the face of this decision you can therefore say only that the Federal Circuit accepted Apex’s appeal and exercised jurisdiction without calling standing into question — i.e., the denial of registration was treated (at least for purposes of this case) as a sufficient basis to litigate the merits on appeal.

What the opinion shows about standing (and what it does not)
- The opinion never articulates or applies an Article III standing analysis (no discussion of injury‑in‑fact, causation, redressability). It simply identifies the parties, recounts the TTAB decision, and then proceeds to review the Board’s DuPont findings (see, e.g., “Apex Bank appeals a decision of the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board…”; “We have jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. § 1295(a)(4)(B).”). That procedural posture is what establishes that the court treated Apex as an appropriate appellant in this case.
- Because the court did not address standing, the opinion does not adopt or announce any rule about what additional injury-in-fact (if any) beyond refusal of registration is required in every case. The only safe, case‑specific conclusion supported by the opinion is that the TTAB’s refusal of registration gave Apex a sufficiently concrete stake for the court to take the appeal and adjudicate the DuPont issues.

How that compares (limited to what the opinion contains)
1) Opposer’s entitlement to bring an opposition under 15 U.S.C. § 1063
- The opinion records that CC Serve “initiated an opposition to Apex’s marks … alleging a likelihood of confusion” (J.A. 2, 6931) and the Board “sustained the opposition under Section 2(d) …” (J.A. 63). It does not discuss statutory “entitlement” theories, Lexmark, or any other Article III framing for opposers. Thus the opinion shows the ordinary sequence (opposer files opposition → Board decides), but it does not speak to the modern Lexmark-style analysis of statutory entitlement vs. Article III injury. You cannot draw from this opinion any new principle about whether an opposer must separately show Article III injury beyond statutory entitlement; the court never considered that question here.

2) Standing in appeals from the PTAB in patent cases
- The opinion contains no discussion of PTAB appeals or the differing standing issues that sometimes arise in patent cases. Nothing in the opinion compares or imports PTAB standing doctrine. Again, the only point the opinion supports is procedural: the Federal Circuit accepted Apex’s appeal from a TTAB Section 2(d) refusal and reached the merits.

Practical takeaway for a law student reading this case
- If you are trying to rely on Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. to argue a general rule about Article III standing for trademark applicants, you cannot do that: the court assumed jurisdiction and resolved the merits without addressing standing. The case is evidence that an applicant whose registration is refused may, at least in practice, obtain review in the Federal Circuit, but it does not explain the constitutional or doctrinal justification for standing. For the doctrinal questions you mention (Lexmark framing of opposer entitlement; how PTAB patent‑appeal standing has been treated), you will need to look to cases that expressly analyze Article III standing; Apex simply does not supply that analysis.

### How can the court call each DuPont factor a question of fact but still identify a 'legal error' in the sixth-factor analysis?
Question:
The opinion says each DuPont factor is a factual question reviewed for substantial evidence, and the overall likelihood of confusion is a question of law. Yet the court vacates the Board’s sixth-factor finding for applying 'too narrow' a similarity scope—a legal flaw. How does the court distinguish between (a) a factual finding about third-party use/registrations and (b) a legal error in defining the scope of 'similar goods' considered under factor six? Is this an application of APA/Chenery principles requiring the agency to apply the correct legal framework to its factfinding?
Answer:
Short answer: The court treated the underlying DuPont factor findings as factual but found a legal error where the Board applied the wrong legal scope for which third‑party marks to consider under factor six. The Board’s decision about what counts as “similar” marks was a legal choice about the proper framework for weighing the third‑party evidence (not a dispute about the existence or weight of particular third‑party uses), so the court vacated and remanded that part of the analysis while leaving factual findings subject to substantial‑evidence review intact.

Why that makes sense (with citations to the opinion)

- Familiar allocation of responsibilities. The opinion starts from the settled division of review: “We review the [B]oard’s legal conclusions de novo, and its findings of fact for substantial evidence.” M2 Software, 450 F.3d at 1382; then it reiterates that “Each of the [thirteen] DuPont factors presents a question of fact” but that “likelihood of confusion is a question of law, based on findings of relevant underlying facts” (Opinion, §II (quoting M2 and Bose)). That framing preserves two levels: (1) factfinding on each factor (substantial‑evidence review), and (2) legal conclusions about the correct legal standards and the court’s ultimate likelihood‑of‑confusion determination (de novo).

- What the Board did as fact vs. what the court called a legal error. The Board made factual findings about third‑party uses — e.g., Apex submitted exhibits showing third‑party uses of “Aspire” (J.A. 40–46), and the Board counted nine Aspire‑formative marks for credit‑card services (J.A. 47). Those are factfinding matters the court would test for substantial evidence. But the court identified a legal error in the Board’s choice of which third‑party marks to consider: the Board “restricted the universe of marks it considered to only those relating to credit card services and excluded marks related to other banking and financing services” (Opinion §III.B, J.A. 6–7). The court characterized that as a legal flaw because it rested on the Board’s narrowing legal definition of “similar” goods for purposes of factor six rather than on a contested factual determination about particular third‑party uses.

- Why narrowing scope is legal (not factual) for purposes of review. The court explains that the Board had already found—factually under factor two—that the parties’ services are “highly similar” and even “partially legally identical” (J.A. 27–28). Given that factual finding, the Board’s decision to treat only credit‑card marks as relevant under factor six was a misapplication of the legal standard that factor six requires consideration of “similar” (not identical) goods. The opinion relies on precedent stating that factor six “does not require identical goods—only similar ones” (citing Olde Tyme Foods; Juice Generation) and concludes that “the Board’s definition of similarity [was] too narrow” (J.A. 7). That is a legal determination about the proper analytic scope, so the court vacated the Board’s factor‑six finding and remanded for reconsideration consistent with the Board’s own factor‑two factual finding.

- The chaining of fact and law. Put differently: the facts found under factor two (services highly similar) change the applicable legal frame for factor six (what counts as “similar goods” to trigger consideration of third‑party marks). The court remanded so the Board can apply the correct legal framework (broader universe of potentially similar third‑party marks) when re‑weighing the third‑party evidence it already has or may collect. That is why the court left the substantial‑evidence finding on factor two undisturbed but vacated factor six as a legal error.

- Relation to agency‑law principles. Although the opinion does not cite Chenery or the APA by name, the court’s action is consistent with the same basic agency‑law principle: an administrative decision must apply the correct legal standard to the facts. The opinion explicitly treats the Board’s restriction as a “legal flaw” and remands for reconsideration in light of its factual finding (J.A. 7). In practice, that is the same corrective mechanism the courts use when an agency’s legal framework is incorrect even though the evidentiary record otherwise contains factual findings.

Bottom line: The court distinguished the Board’s factual findings about specific third‑party uses (reviewed for substantial evidence) from the Board’s legal choice about how narrowly to define “similar” goods when applying factor six. The latter was a legal error because it misapplied the legal standard (especially in light of the Board’s own factual finding that the parties’ services are highly similar), so the court vacated and remanded that part of the analysis while leaving the factual findings intact.

### Why must the similarity scope used for factor two carry over to factor six?
Question:
The Board found the parties’ services 'highly similar' and 'legally identical, in part' under factor two, but then limited the relevant public and third-party marks to 'credit card services' only under factor six. The Federal Circuit says that was error and the Board should 'retain the same scope' when evaluating similarity under other factors. What doctrinal principle requires this consistency across factors? Is it that 'similar goods' has one legal meaning across DuPont factors, or that inconsistent scoping is arbitrary under the APA?
Answer:
Short answer: The Federal Circuit requires consistency because the DuPont factors rest on the same underlying factual finding about what goods/services are “similar,” so once the Board defines the scope of similarity for factor two it must use that same scope when asking whether third‑party marks are used on “similar” goods for factor six. That is a doctrinal consistency rule rooted in (1) the way the DuPont framework works—factual findings about similarity supply the universe for other factors—and (2) the Court’s review obligations (an inconsistent narrowing is legally flawed and effectively arbitrary on the record).

Why: what the opinion actually says
- The Court affirms the Board’s factual finding under factor two that Apex’s “banking and financing services” and CC Serve’s “credit card services” are “highly similar” and even “legally identical, in part.” J.A. 27–28, 33–34.
- The Court then faults the Board for, in the factor six analysis, restricting the relevant third‑party marks to just those tied to “credit card services” while excluding marks covering other banking and financing services. The Court calls that “an error” and explains: “When the Board has already made a factual finding that the services are highly similar . . . the Board should retain the same scope in its consideration of similarity under the other factors. We see no reason to impose a different and more stringent legal standard for similarity under the sixth DuPont factor.” J.A. 6–7 (¶ beginning “That was an error.”).

Doctrinal content (concise)
- The DuPont analysis is a multi‑factor legal test built on common underlying facts (the similarity of goods/services). Once the Board defines what the services encompass for factor two, that factual definition supplies the proper universe of “similar goods/services” for other DuPont inquiries—e.g., whether third‑party uses show weakness under factor six.
- The Court cites precedent that factor six requires consideration of similar (not identical) goods, so narrowing the universe below the Board’s own finding of similarity is improper (Olde Tyme Foods; Juice Generation). See J.A. 7 (citing those cases).
- Practically, the error is both a substantive misapplication of the DuPont framework and a legal/arbitrary error subject to reversal: the Board applied a different (more stringent) standard to factor six than it applied to factor two, which is legally inconsistent and therefore vacated. The opinion frames the problem as legal error in the Board’s analysis (and remands for reconsideration), which is the same sort of inconsistency the APA review standard will correct on appeal.

So: it is not a metaphysical claim that “similar goods” has one rigid statutory definition that must mean the same thing in every DuPont factor. Rather, the Court requires that the Board consistently apply its established factual definition of the parties’ goods/services across the DuPont analysis. If the Board departs from that scope without a reasoned basis, that departure is legal error (and effectively arbitrary) under appellate review. The opinion ties both points together: the DuPont framework depends on consistent factual findings, and inconsistent scoping here was a legal/arbritrariness flaw that warranted vacatur and remand (J.A. 6–7).

### How does the Board properly interpret an identification like 'credit card services' when the ID Manual entry has been deleted since registration?
Question:
The Board used dictionaries to elaborate 'credit card services' after noting the Trademark ID Manual entry was deleted post-registration, then treated various credit-card-related activities as within that identification. Is it proper to rely on current dictionaries or the now-deleted Manual entry to interpret the scope of a decades-old registration? Do TTAB principles require construing identifications as of the registration date, and if so, how is that reconciled with evolving ID Manual entries and terminology?
Answer:
Short answer
- The Board is required to determine what the wording of the earlier registration actually covers and may rely on ordinary‑meaning sources (dictionaries, common usage, the registration file and evidence of how the mark is used) to do so. Where an ID Manual entry that existed at or after registration has been deleted post‑registration, the Board may look to dictionary definitions and other evidence to define the services in the registration. The Federal Circuit accepted that approach here (the Board defined “credit card services” by reference to dictionaries and evidence and the court affirmed the Board’s substantive finding on the second DuPont factor).  
- One caveat the court emphasized: once the Board fixes the scope of services for purposes of the second DuPont factor, it must remain consistent when analyzing other factors (the court vacated the Board’s sixth‑factor analysis because the Board then narrowed the universe of third‑party marks inconsistently).

What the opinion says (specifics)
- The Board recognized that the Trademark ID Manual entry for “credit card services” had been deleted after CC Serve’s registration, so it “first determined the meaning of the identified services.” The Board concluded that “credit card services” includes issuing credit cards, counseling about credit card debt, processing payments/transactions, authorization, monitoring and alerts, managing accounts, and providing access to credit scores. (Opinion cites J.A.25.)
- The Board then relied on dictionary definitions for “banking,” “bank,” and “finance” showing those words can encompass extending credit or providing funds through issuance of credit cards, and concluded Apex’s “banking and financing services” overlap with CC Serve’s services and are “legally identical, in part.” (J.A.27–28.) The Federal Circuit affirmed that factual finding and the Board’s conclusion on the second DuPont factor (J.A.35).

Legal doctrine and how it applies here
- The central task is to determine what the earlier registration’s identification actually covers. The Board must interpret the plain meaning of the words of the identification in the registration record and may use external, ordinary‑meaning sources (dictionaries, industry usage, evidence of actual services offered under the mark, and third‑party registrations/usages) to do so. That is what the Board did here after noting the ID Manual entry had been deleted.
- The opinion approves that approach in practice: the Board defined the deleted/manually‑changed identification by consulting dictionaries and the record and the Federal Circuit found substantial evidence supported the Board’s finding that the parties’ services are highly similar (affirming the second DuPont factor). (See J.A.25, J.A.27–28, J.A.35.)
- However, the Board must be consistent in its chosen scope. The court criticized the Board for using one scope when deciding the second DuPont factor (finding services highly similar / partially legally identical) and then constricting the universe of third‑party uses when analyzing the sixth DuPont factor. The Federal Circuit held the sixth‑factor inquiry should encompass marks used on similar goods/services as the Board already found—i.e., the Board should not apply a narrower definition under later factors than it applied in the second‑factor analysis (see J.A.27–28, J.A.47–48).

Practical takeaways for the Board and practitioners
- If the ID Manual entry has been deleted since registration, expect the Board to define the identification by reference to the ordinary meaning of the words and supporting evidence (dictionaries, industry practice, the registrant’s actual usage and third‑party evidence). That method was upheld here for the second DuPont factor. (J.A.25–28, J.A.35.)
- Preserve and produce contemporaneous evidence of meaning/use at or near the registration/priority date if you want the Board to construe the identification narrowly or as it was understood historically; absent that, the Board will rely on ordinary meanings and present evidence. (The opinion shows the Board filled the gap left by the deleted Manual entry with dictionaries and usage evidence.)
- Be consistent: once the Board fixes the scope of the registration’s services for one DuPont factor, it should apply the same scope in evaluating other factors (the court remanded because the Board narrowed the relevant universe when considering third‑party uses). (J.A.27–28, J.A.47–51.)

Bottom line
The Board acted within acceptable practice by interpreting a registration whose ID Manual entry had been deleted through dictionaries and record evidence and the Federal Circuit affirmed that interpretation for the second DuPont factor. But the Board must stick to the scope it adopts across the DuPont analysis—an inconsistent narrowing (as occurred here in the sixth‑factor analysis) will be vacated and remanded.

### What evidentiary weight do third-party registrations vs. third-party use carry in proving weakness under factor six, and is there any quantitative threshold?
Question:
The Board discounted broader third-party 'Aspire' uses and found only nine relevant credit-card uses, concluding that number did not show weakness. The Federal Circuit doesn’t set a number but flags the Board’s too-narrow scope. Practically, how should parties prove weakness—do registrations alone help, or is marketplace use necessary? Are cases like Juice Generation (26 marks) and Jack Wolfskin (14 notable examples) setting any benchmark, or is the inquiry more qualitative (e.g., proximity of services, geographic spread, consumer exposure)?
Answer:
Short answer
- The Federal Circuit in Apex Bank does not adopt any numeric cutoff. The court criticized the Board for using an unduly narrow scope of “credit card” marks and for treating only nine third‑party credit‑card uses as insufficient. It directed the Board to consider all third‑party marks for similar services consistent with its finding on the second DuPont factor.
- Quantitative examples in cases such as Juice Generation (26 third‑party marks) and Jack Wolfskin (14 “notable” examples) are persuasive illustrations of a “considerable” number, not formal benchmarks. The inquiry remains both quantitative and qualitative: number matters, but so do nature, geographic spread, consumer exposure, and closeness of the services.
- Practically, registrations help but are weaker proof than marketplace use. Parties should present a mix: registrations plus concrete evidence of third‑party commercial use (advertising, sales, websites, customers, geographic reach, duration) tied to the same relevant consumer universe the Board adopts under the second factor.

Explanation and authorities (grounded in the Apex opinion)
1) Apex’s holding about scope and numbers
- Apex vacated the Board’s sixth‑factor finding because the Board considered only marks tied narrowly to “credit card services” even though it had already found the parties’ services “highly similar” and partly legally identical under the second DuPont factor. The court held that once the Board finds high similarity, it must use that same scope when evaluating third‑party marks under factor six. (Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp., J.A. 27–28, 33–34, 47–48.)
- The court did not create a numeric rule. It remanded for the Board to reassess factor six with the appropriate scope rather than substituting any specific numeric threshold. (Apex opinion, vacating factor six and remanding.)

2) Role of quantity vs. quality
- The opinion expressly cites Juice Generation and Jack Wolfskin as examples of courts describing “a considerable number” of third‑party marks (26 in Juice Generation, 14 notable examples in Jack Wolfskin), but treats those numbers as descriptive precedent, not as a rigid threshold. (Apex, J.A. 47 (citing Juice Generation, 794 F.3d at 1337 n.1; Jack Wolfskin, 797 F.3d at 1373).)
- The Federal Circuit’s precedent (also cited in Apex: Palm Bay, Olde Tyme) shows that factor six is about whether the field is “crowded” enough that consumers are accustomed to distinguishing similar marks. That is inherently a mixed quantitative/qualitative inquiry: number of marks is relevant, but so is the nature of the marks and how consumers encounter them. (Apex at J.A. 46–47; Palm Bay Imps., 396 F.3d at 1373; Olde Tyme Foods, 961 F.2d at 204.)

3) Registrations vs. marketplace use
- Registrations are admissible and relevant evidence of third‑party adoption of a mark (Apex itself relied on third‑party registrations in its second‑factor analysis, J.A. 33–34), but registrations alone are weaker proof of consumer exposure and commercial weakness. Courts often give greater weight to evidence of actual marketplace use (sales, advertising, consumer recognition) because factor six asks about “marks in use on similar goods.”
- Apex reflects this balance: the Board relied on a count of nine credit‑card uses and treated them as insufficiently “considerable,” yet the court faulted the Board for limiting scope rather than for relying on registrations or uses per se. The court’s remedy was to require the Board to consider a broader set of marks (those for similar banking/financial services) and reassess their weight. (Apex at J.A. 46–48.)

4) Practical proof strategy (what to submit and how to frame it)
- Match the scope to the second DuPont finding: if the Board (or you) establishes that services are highly similar or overlap, include third‑party marks for the full range of similar services (not just a narrow subcategory).
- Combine evidence types:
  - Registrations covering the relevant services (shows third parties chose similar marks).
  - Concrete marketplace use: screenshots, websites, advertising, catalogs, sales figures, press releases, dealer networks, dates of first use, and geographic distribution.
  - Evidence of consumer exposure/recognition: web analytics, media impressions, trade press, or (if available) survey evidence.
  - Evidence on closeness of services: how consumers purchase/use the services, channels of trade, and whether the same consumers encounter the third‑party marks.
- Emphasize qualitative factors in briefing: similarity of goods/services, consumer overlap, duration and prominence of third‑party use, multi‑jurisdictional reach, and whether third parties operate in the same channels where confusion would occur.
- Use Juice Generation/Jack Wolfskin as persuasive analogues: point to their numbers and show how your combination of quantity + quality compares, but do not treat those numbers as required thresholds.

Conclusion
Apex confirms there is no magic numeric cutoff; courts look at both how many similar marks exist and how meaningful those marks are in the marketplace. Registrations matter and should be submitted, but they are most effective when paired with evidence of actual use, consumer exposure, and service proximity consistent with the second DuPont factor. Juice Generation and Jack Wolfskin illustrate when courts have found the field sufficiently crowded, but the determination remains fact‑specific and qualitative as well as quantitative.

### Is defining the 'relevant public' for factor six a legal determination or a factual finding?
Question:
The Board narrowed the relevant public to 'consumers of credit card services' and excluded marks used for other banking/financing services; the Federal Circuit called this 'legally flawed.' When the Board identifies the relevant public for assessing third-party use, is that treated as a question of fact, a mixed question, or a pure legal issue about the proper scope of comparison under DuPont? What guidance governs that scoping decision?
Answer:
Short answer: It’s primarily a factual determination (reviewed for substantial evidence) but governed and constrained by legal standards — i.e., a classic mixed-question situation: the Board must find the “relevant public” as a factual matter, but it must apply the legal rule that the sixth DuPont factor looks to similar (not necessarily identical) goods/services and must be consistent with its other DuPont findings. The Federal Circuit reversed here because the Board applied the legal standard too narrowly and inconsistently with its own factual finding on factor two.

Why — explained with the opinion

- Standard of review the court states: “We review the [B]oard’s legal conclusions de novo, and its findings of fact for substantial evidence.” M2 Software, 450 F.3d at 1382 (quoted at J.A. 3). The court also reminds that “Each of the [thirteen] DuPont factors presents a question of fact,” (Bose, quoted at J.A. 3). That framework controls how the scoping decision is treated on appeal.

- The Board’s task is factual: the Board here defined the “relevant public” to be “consumers of ‘credit card services’” and limited its sixth-factor inquiry to third‑party uses in that narrow universe (J.A. 47). The Federal Circuit treated that as a factual scope choice that must be supported by the record (i.e., substantial evidence), because the DuPont factors depend on underlying factual findings.

- The court held the Board’s scope was legally flawed because of the legal rule governing the sixth factor: the sixth factor “does not require identical goods—only similar ones” (citing Olde Tyme and Juice Generation; holding summarized at J.A. 6–7). Because the Board had already found (as a factual matter under factor two) that the parties’ services are “highly similar, in fact, partially legally identical” (J.A. 27–28), the Board could not then constrict factor six to a more stringent, narrower definition of similarity. The court said: “When the Board has already made a factual finding that the services are highly similar . . . the Board should retain the same scope in its consideration of similarity under the other factors. We see no reason to impose a different and more stringent legal standard for similarity under the sixth DuPont factor.” (J.A. 7.)

What this means as a rule of decision

- The Board must make a factual determination of the “relevant public”/scope of goods or services it will consider under factor six, and that finding is tested on appeal for substantial evidence.

- But the Board’s factual scope determination is constrained by legal doctrines:
  - Sixth DuPont factor requires consideration of marks used on similar (not strictly identical) goods/services (Olde Tyme; Juice Generation — cited and applied at J.A. 6–7).
  - The Board must be consistent: it cannot adopt one scope for factor two (finding high similarity) and then adopt a narrower legal standard for factor six. The scope used under different DuPont factors should be coherent with the Board’s factual findings (J.A. 7).

- On appeal courts will:
  - Review the legal standard applied (e.g., whether the Board required identical goods when law requires similarity) de novo.
  - Review the Board’s factual scope decision for substantial evidence (i.e., whether the record supports the Board’s chosen “relevant public”).

Practical takeaways for the Board (and litigants)

- When the Board defines the “relevant public” for factor six, it should (1) articulate the factual basis for what customers/services are “relevant,” (2) apply the settled legal rule that similarity, not identity, suffices, and (3) ensure the scope is consistent with any prior factual findings about similarity under other DuPont factors (exactly the inconsistency the court reversed here—compare J.A. 27–28 with the Board’s narrow scope at J.A. 47).

In short: scoping is a factual finding subject to substantial-evidence review, but it must apply the correct legal standard (similarity, not identity) and be consistent with the Board’s other DuPont findings; failure to do so is reversible as happened in this opinion (see J.A. 6–7).

### How did the Board justify treating non-bank credit-card program management as 'legally identical, in part' to 'banking and financing services'?
Question:
Apex argued CC Serve partners with banks but doesn’t itself provide 'banking services.' The Board looked to dictionary definitions of 'banking' and 'finance' (including extending credit/issuing cards) and concluded the services are highly similar/legally identical in part. Is this consistent with TTAB precedent that the identification of services controls regardless of the actual business model and trade channels? How do tribunals handle situations where a registrant’s role (program manager vs issuer) differs from the ordinary meaning of 'banking' while the applicant’s identification is broader?
Answer:
Short answer
The Board treated CC Serve’s “credit card services” as overlapping with Apex’s identified “banking and financing services” because it looked to the ordinary meaning of the identifications (and dictionary definitions) and concluded those ordinary meanings include issuing/extending credit by means of credit cards. Because TTAB precedent generally treats the identification of services as controlling for scope, the Board’s method is consistent with precedent: the Board asked what the identifications mean to the relevant public, not how the parties actually structure their businesses. That does not mean a registrant’s business model is irrelevant—if the registrant can show the ID’s ordinary meaning is narrower (or that real-world trade channels and consumer expectations differ), tribunals will take that into account—but where the ID (as interpreted) covers issuing credit/cards, program-management-only operations will not avoid a finding of overlap.

Explanation and authorities from the opinion

1) What the Board did and why
- The Board first construed the parties’ service identifications. Because “credit card services” had been removed from the ID Manual, the Board defined that term itself: it found the phrase “encompasses ‘issuing credit cards for use to finance purchases as well as counseling regarding credit card debt, processing credit card payments and transactions, credit card authorization, credit card monitoring and alerts, managing credit card accounts, and providing access to credit scores.’” (Decision quoted at J.A. 25.)
- The Board then compared those ordinary-meaning services to Apex’s stated “banking” and “finance” services and determined—based on dictionary definitions that include “extending credit” and “issuing credit cards”—that the services are “legally identical, in part.” (J.A. 27–28.)
- The Board also relied on third‑party registrations that covered both credit card services and banking/financing services to show the services commonly emanate from a single source. (J.A. 33–34.)
- The Federal Circuit affirmed this conclusion as supported by substantial evidence and consistent with the DuPont standard that the services need not be identical, only “related in some manner” or such that their marketing could cause consumers to believe they come from the same source. The court cited Coach Servs., Inc. v. Triumph Learning LLC for that principle and expressly held Apex’s argument that CC Serve merely “partners with banks” to manage programs was “unavailing” because the Board properly examined the service descriptions. (See discussion at J.A. 24–28 and Apex’s argument rejected in III.A.)

2) How this fits TTAB/precedent about identifications controlling
- TTAB and Federal Circuit practice focus on the scope of the identification of services/ goods when assessing similarity. The Board’s approach—construing the IDs’ ordinary meanings and comparing those meanings—follows that controlling principle. The opinion repeatedly treats the ID text and dictionary/registration evidence as the baseline for what consumers understand the services to be (see J.A. 25–28, 33–34).
- The court likewise emphasized that the services “need not be identical” but may be “legally identical, in part” if the ordinary meanings overlap (J.A. 24–28). That is consistent with the principle that the ID controls unless there is evidence to narrow it.

3) When a registrant’s actual business model differs (program manager vs. issuer)
- Practical consequence shown by the opinion: a registrant’s internal role (e.g., program manager that partners with a bank) does not automatically narrow the scope of its registration if the registration’s identification (or ordinary meaning) encompasses activities such as issuing or managing card accounts. The Board here treated CC Serve’s services as encompassing account management and card‑issuance‑related activities (J.A. 25, 27–28) even though CC Serve contracts with a bank for issuance.
- That said, tribunals do accept and weigh evidence about how services are actually provided when such evidence meaningfully narrows consumer perception. The Board considered third‑party registrations and use, and the Federal Circuit remanded other issues (DuPont factors one and six) to re-evaluate strength and commercial impression in light of the Board’s service-construction. That shows two limits:
  - If a party can present convincing evidence (specimens, declarations, consumer testimony, industry practice, advertising/trade channels) showing that the public perceives its services as materially narrower or different than the ordinary meaning of the ID, the Board may adopt a narrower construction.
  - If the identification itself is broad, mere proof of a particular internal contractual role (e.g., program manager) may be insufficient—unless the evidence ties to consumer perception or the ordinary meaning of the ID.

Practical lessons for litigants or examiners
- If you want narrow protection, draft a narrow identification that excludes activities you do not perform (e.g., “program management for bank‑issued credit card accounts, excluding card issuance”). The ID controls at examination/opposition.
- If defending a registration whose ID is broad but whose business model is narrow, introduce evidence that consumers do not view the services as overlapping with the applicant’s identified services (consumer surveys, specimens, industry declarations, trade-channel evidence).
- If attacking an application, show that the applicant’s identification, as commonly understood, overlaps with the registrant’s ID (dictionary meanings, industry practice, third‑party registrations)—exactly the steps the Board took here (J.A. 25–28, 33–34).

Bottom line
The Board’s treatment was consistent with TTAB/Federal Circuit practice: start with the identification’s ordinary meaning, use dictionary and registration evidence to construe that meaning, and compare the identifications. A registrant’s internal role can be relevant, but to avoid overlap a registrant must either have a narrower ID or produce record evidence that the ordinary meaning (and the relevant public’s perception) does not encompass the applicant’s identified services. The Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s service‑construction here and rejected Apex’s “program manager not a bank” argument because the record supported the Board’s reading of the IDs. (See J.A. 24–28, 33–35.)

### Why did the court vacate the first DuPont factor based on potential re-evaluation of commercial strength under factor six?
Question:
The panel says commercial impression under factor one is informed by the mark’s commercial strength/weakness, so it vacates factor one because factor six might change on remand. Is the court treating conceptual/commercial strength as a necessary input to commercial impression? In practice, how interdependent are these factors—can the Board ever decide factor one purely on appearance/sound/connotation without knowing strength, or is that always an abuse of discretion/legal error?
Answer:
Short answer
The court vacated the Board’s DuPont factor one finding because the Board’s flawed factor six analysis could change its assessment of the mark’s commercial strength, and commercial strength is a relevant input to the “commercial impression” inquiry. The panel did not hold that every factor‑one determination must always await a full strength inquiry; rather, when strength (factor six) is both contested and potentially outcome‑determinative of how the public will perceive the mark, it is proper — and sometimes required — to reassess factor one after resolving factor six.

Why the court did this (doctrine and the opinion’s reasoning)
- DuPont factor one asks about similarity “in their entireties as to appearance, sound, connotation and commercial impression.” The phrase “commercial impression” imports not only raw appearance/sound/meaning but also how consumers will perceive the mark in marketplace context.
- Factor six (third‑party use) is a principal means of showing a mark’s commercial weakness or strength. As the panel explains, evidence of third‑party uses can show a field is “crowded” and that consumers are “educated to distinguish between marks,” which reduces a mark’s scope of protection. (Opinion §III.B, citing Palm Bay, Juice Generation, Jack Wolfskin.)
- The panel found the Board erred by narrowing the universe of third‑party marks considered under factor six even though it had already found the parties’ services to be “highly similar.” Because a different, broader consideration of third‑party uses might lead to a different conclusion about CC Serve’s commercial strength, the court concluded that the Board’s factor‑one “commercial impression” assessment could be affected and therefore vacated factor one for reconsideration. (Opinion §III.B–C, J.A. 47, 51, 7.)

How interdependent are the factors in practice?
- They are related but analytically distinct. Factor one is essentially a side‑by‑side comparison of the marks’ appearance, sound, connotation and overall commercial impression; factor six (third‑party use) is evidence used to gauge how strong or weak the senior mark is in the marketplace.
- In many cases you can evaluate factor one on the bases of appearance/sound/meaning alone (e.g., where strength is undisputed, irrelevant, or trivial). The Board and courts often do so. But commercial strength is a factual circumstance that can—and sometimes does—alter the net “commercial impression.” For example:
  - A very strong, unique mark may be perceived by consumers as a single source so that even modest similarities in appearance/meaning produce confusion.
  - A weak or crowded mark may be perceived by consumers as describing a class of goods or as one of many similar marks, making even moderately similar marks less likely to cause confusion.
- Thus, when strength is meaningfully contested and the record evidence on factor six could realistically change how consumers are expected to perceive the mark, resolving factor six first (or at least reassessing factor one after resolving factor six) is not only permissible but required to avoid an incomplete analysis. That is exactly what the panel did here: because the Board limited the third‑party universe improperly, the court could not be sure the Board’s commercial‑impression finding was correct absent a proper factor‑six analysis. (Opinion §III.B–C.)

Practical rule of thumb for Board decisions and appeals
- If third‑party use/strength evidence is weak, undisputed, or plainly irrelevant to how consumers will understand the mark, the Board can decide factor one on appearance/sound/connotation alone.
- If third‑party use/strength is disputed and could materially change whether consumers view the mark as strong/unique or weak/crowded, the Board should fully analyze factor six and then (re)assess factor one in light of that strength determination. Failure to do so risks vacatur for an incomplete likelihood‑of‑confusion analysis (as the panel remedied here). (See Opinion §§III.B–C.)

Bottom line
The court treated commercial strength as a potentially necessary input to the commercial‑impression inquiry only because factor six could materially change the marketplace perception of the ASPIRE mark. It did not announce a per se rule that factor one always requires prior resolution of factor six; rather, it required the Board to correct its defective factor‑six analysis and then reassess factor one because strength was contested and relevant to commercial impression in this case.

### Does a § 2(d) refusal raise any First Amendment concerns after Tam and Brunetti?
Question:
The opinion doesn’t mention constitutional issues, but I’m wondering whether denying registration for likelihood of confusion implicates speech. After Tam/Brunetti struck viewpoint-based bars, is § 2(d) considered a content-neutral, source-identification regulation tied to preventing consumer confusion, and thus constitutional? Are there cases explicitly holding that § 2(d) refusals do not offend the First Amendment because they regulate registration (not use) and serve substantial/compelling interests?
Answer:
Short answer: No — Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. does not address First Amendment issues. The opinion treats §2(d) as a straightforward likelihood-of-confusion refusal under the DuPont framework and contains no constitutional analysis or holding that §2(d) is (or is not) subject to First Amendment scrutiny after Tam and Brunetti.

Why: what this opinion actually does
- The panel frames the dispute purely as a §2(d) likelihood-of-confusion case and reviews the Board’s factual findings under the DuPont factors. See slip op. §§II–III (citing DuPont, M2 Software, Bose). The Court affirms the Board on the second DuPont factor, vacates and remands as to the first and sixth factors, and otherwise confines its decision to trademark-law doctrine and substantial-evidence review. See slip op. pp. 4–12 (affirmed-in-part, vacated-in-part, remanded).
- Nothing in the opinion engages First Amendment doctrine, Tam v. Matal, or Iancu v. Brunetti. The Court does not treat registration refusals as government speech, nor does it analyze whether §2(d) is content- or viewpoint-based, nor does it consider any compelled-speech or prior-restraint problems.

What follows from that (legal takeaways for your question)
- Apex leaves open the constitutional question. Because the opinion never addresses Tam/Brunetti or any free‑speech challenge, it provides no precedent that §2(d) refusals do or do not raise First Amendment problems.
- Substantively, the opinion anchors its analysis to the traditional source‑identification/likelihood‑of‑confusion regime (DuPont factors). If you are thinking doctrinally, the opinion treats §2(d) as a classical trademark refusal doctrine aimed at preventing consumer confusion (see slip op. §II and discussion of the DuPont factors in §III). But the Court does not say whether that regulatory aim renders §2(d) constitutionally permissible post‑Tam/Brunetti.

Practical consequence for litigants/researchers
- You cannot cite Apex for the proposition that §2(d) is immune from First Amendment attack. Apex is useful only for its application of DuPont (e.g., the Court’s treatment of the second, sixth, and first factors and the limits on the Board’s scope of third‑party evidence). See slip op. §§III.A–C.
- If you want binding authority on whether §2(d) raises First Amendment concerns after Tam/Brunetti, you will need to look to other decisions that expressly analyze constitutional issues (Apex does not do so).

If helpful, next steps
- If you want, I can compile and summarize cases (post‑Tam/Brunetti) that directly address First Amendment challenges to §2(d) refusals, so you can see how courts are treating registration refusals (registration versus use, content‑neutrality, government speech, commercial‑speech tests, etc.).

### On remand, can the Board reopen the record to consider additional third-party evidence, or is it limited to the existing record?
Question:
The Federal Circuit vacates factor six (and thus one) for legal error in scoping. Procedurally, what happens next at the TTAB—does it reassess the sixth and first factors on the existing record, or can the parties introduce new evidence of third-party registrations/uses consistent with the broader 'similar services' scope? What rules or precedents govern record reopening on remand from the Federal Circuit in TTAB appeals?
Answer:
Short answer
The Federal Circuit’s opinion does not lock the Board into the existing administrative record. The court vacated the Board’s sixth‑factor and (consequently) first‑factor findings and remanded for reconsideration “in light of” the Board’s factual finding that the parties’ services are highly similar (see J.A. 27–28, 33–34, 51–52). That language contemplates that the Board must reexamine the sixth (and then the first) factor with the proper scope of “similar services” and therefore may consider third‑party registrations/uses that are relevant to that broader scope. Practically, a party seeking to add third‑party evidence on remand should move the Board for leave to reopen the record and explain the relevance of the new material to the broadened similarity determination.

Why (and what the opinion says)
- The Court affirmed the Board’s finding that the parties’ services are “highly similar” and even “partially legally identical.” J.A. 27–28, 33–34. That factual finding is the premise for vacating the sixth‑factor ruling: the Board had limited its sixth‑factor inquiry only to marks used on credit‑card services, but the Court held that, given the Board’s own finding about service similarity, the universe of comparable third‑party marks should not have been so narrowly defined. J.A. 47–48, 51–52.
- The Court therefore “vacate[d] the Board’s finding with respect to the sixth DuPont factor and remand[ed] for reconsideration of the appropriate scope of third‑party marks eligible for consideration in view of the Board’s factual finding that the parties’ services are highly similar.” J.A. 51–52 (emphasis added). The Court also vacated the first factor because the commercial‑strength inquiry under factor six could affect the marks’ “commercial impression.” J.A. 52.

What that means procedurally at the TTAB
- The Board must reconsider factors six and one consistent with the Court’s instructions: use the same scope of “similar services” adopted under factor two when assessing which third‑party marks are “similar” for factor six, and then reassess commercial impression under factor one in light of that result. J.A. 27–28, 51–52.
- The opinion does not expressly prohibit the Board from admitting additional third‑party evidence on remand. Because the Court remanded for a proper scope determination of marks “eligible for consideration,” the Board will need to consider whatever evidence is properly before it that bears on that broader universe of similar marks. In other words, the remedial instruction contemplates a broader evidentiary inquiry than the one the Board performed.

Practical consequences and recommended procedure (what parties should do)
- A party who wishes the Board to consider new third‑party registrations/uses should promptly move the Board for leave to reopen the record on remand and submit the proposed evidence with a proffer explaining its relevance to the broader “similar services” scope the Court directed the Board to apply (i.e., show how the new third‑party material relates to the services the Board already found highly similar). The Court’s remand makes clear that such material is the kind of evidence that could affect factors six and one. See J.A. 51–52.
- The opposing party should be given an opportunity to respond to any newly proffered third‑party evidence so the Board can make fact findings with a complete and adversarially tested record before issuing its new DuPont determinations.
- If neither party requests reopening, the Board still must reweigh factors one and six in light of the Court’s instruction using the evidence already of record. But the Board should be alert that the Court’s remand contemplates a different scope for the universe of third‑party marks, and the Board must explain why it excludes or includes particular third‑party materials if it relies only on the existing record.

Bottom line
The Federal Circuit’s remand requires the Board to apply a broader “similar services” lens when reconsidering DuPont factors six and one (see J.A. 27–28, 51–52). That reconsideration may legitimately include additional third‑party registrations or uses relevant to that broader scope, but such evidence should be placed before the Board by motion for leave to reopen the record (with supporting explanation) so that the Board can consider it and give the opposing party an opportunity to respond.

